WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.667
The model that got worse overnight.

2
00:00:02.967 --> 00:00:06.074
Nothing was deployed, yet the output is garbage.

3
00:00:06.074 --> 00:00:08.533
Let's debug it like a senior engineer.

4
00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:10.867
It's Monday morning.

5
00:00:11.167 --> 00:00:16.333
Your pipeline uses an LLM to extract structured fields from customer emails.

6
00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:18.700
Support says it's returning garbage.

7
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.233
And nothing has been deployed since Thursday.

8
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:25.100
Nothing changed is almost always false.

9
00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:27.562
Something outside your repo changed.

10
00:00:27.562 --> 00:00:29.067
List every hypothesis.

11
00:00:29.367 --> 00:00:32.633
The provider's alias may have moved to a new model version.

12
00:00:32.933 --> 00:00:34.433
Your inputs may have shifted.

13
00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:37.700
An upstream parser may now include HTML.

14
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.900
Longer inputs may be truncated.

15
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.367
Timeouts may fall back to a weaker model.

16
00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:44.067
Or nothing broke at all.

17
00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:46.867
Now find the cheapest test.

18
00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:50.800
Take twenty inputs from last Wednesday that worked, and run them now.

19
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.938
If they still work, the model is fine and your inputs shifted.

20
00:00:54.938 --> 00:00:57.848
If they fail, the model or its version changed.

21
00:00:57.848 --> 00:00:58.900
That's bisecting.

22
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.667
And what should have existed so you'd know on Friday?

23
00:01:02.967 --> 00:01:05.867
Pinned model versions, never floating aliases.

24
00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:11.033
A daily canary eval of thirty golden examples, with an alert when the score drops.

25
00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:15.000
Input monitoring for lengths, schema failures, and new sources.

26
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:19.100
And tracing, so you can compare an old good trace with a new bad one.

27
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:22.700
The trap is concluding the model got dumber.

28
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.478
That is a hypothesis, not a conclusion.

29
00:01:25.478 --> 00:01:29.100
Bisect first, and blame vendors only with a diff in hand.

30
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.092
Let's recap.

31
00:01:31.092 --> 00:01:33.667
Nothing changed is almost always false.

32
00:01:33.967 --> 00:01:36.033
Rerun old good inputs to bisect.

33
00:01:36.333 --> 00:01:38.600
Pin versions and run a daily canary.

34
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.970
Blame the model only with a diff.

35
00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:43.667
Next up: the client who wants to fine-tune.
